- op: discuss  # a26c
  at: '2026-04-07T21:28:50Z'  # a26c
  by: agent  # a26c
  actor: jgstern_agent  # a26c
  clock: 6  # a26c
  nonce: a26c  # a26c
  message: "You're right to surface this. As of 2026-04-07 we have at least 6 per-language YAML config sections that affect analyzer behavior:\n\n  - dataflow.yaml — assignments/returns positional rules + library_patterns (PRs #2733, WI-hivud, WI-supih, WI-fogis)\n  - framework_patterns YAML — route detection, lifecycle hooks, manifest discovery (docs/FRAMEWORKS.md cross-refs ~150 frameworks)\n  - test framework detection patterns (PR fixed cargo-test false positive)\n  - signatures.yaml — proposed by ADR-0006 augmentation (PR #2771) for the INV-dihos return-type registry\n  - type_hierarchy per-language flags — proposed in this WI's earlier message for default_concrete_extends_dispatches (the A1 framing)\n  - io_boundary patterns — implicit in io_boundary.py\n\nThe user-discoverability problem is real: a developer who installs hypergumbo from PyPI and runs it on a Kotlin repo has no way to see 'these are the rules hypergumbo uses to interpret your code' without grepping the wheel.\n\nTHREE PROPOSALS for tracking/surfacing, ordered from cheapest to deepest:\n\n(P1) 'hypergumbo config show <lang>' subcommand (cheap, ~1 day): print every YAML section that applies to <lang> as merged JSON, plus the file path each section came from. 'hypergumbo config show kotlin' would dump dataflow rules + framework patterns + type hierarchy flags + signature extraction rules + io boundary patterns side-by-side. Discoverable from --help, no docs site needed. This is the minimum to address 'I have no way to see what hypergumbo thinks Kotlin looks like'.\n\n(P2) Auto-generated reference page (medium, ~1 week): a build-time script walks every '*.yaml' under packages/hypergumbo-lang-*/configs/ and emits docs/REFERENCE_PER_LANGUAGE.md with a section per language listing every rule, its source file, and an example. Linked from README. Same data as P1 but offline-readable on PyPI/Codeberg without running the tool. Could also produce a per-language stats summary (e.g., 'Go: 30 dataflow rules, 12 frameworks, 8 routing patterns').\n\n(P3) ADR + cross-cutting registry (deeper, ~2 weeks): an ADR documenting the 'per-language config surface' as a first-class concept, plus a 'language_profile.py' registry in core that loads, merges, and exposes every applicable rule for a language as a single object. Analyzers consume the registry instead of reading YAML themselves. This is the long-term direction if we end up with 10+ config sections per language. ADR would also define the contribution surface ('how do I add a rule for my language?') so external contributors can submit YAML diffs without touching Python.\n\nRECOMMENDATION: ship P1 as a small standalone WI now (cheap, immediately solves the user-facing discoverability problem), defer P2 to after Phases 1-2 of the INV-dihos rollout (so the signatures.yaml docs can be generated alongside the dataflow/framework docs), and only adopt P3 if the per-language config surface continues to grow beyond what 'config show' can usefully display.\n\nFor the immediate WI-sukav decision, the per-language flag table (A1 framing) is still the right immediate fix — even with no config surfacing infrastructure, the table is small (~20 lines), lives in one file, and follows the same pattern as the dataflow/framework rules. The discoverability work tracked above should not block A1 from shipping.\n\nQUESTIONS BACK TO YOU:\n1. Should I create a separate WI for P1 ('hypergumbo config show <lang>') now and proceed with the A1 fix here?\n2. Or do you want me to hold A1 until the discoverability story is settled?\n3. Is P3 worth even an exploratory ADR, or is it premature given that we only have 6 config sections?"  # a26c
